COMMAND NAME: gpinitsystem

Initializes a Greenplum Database system by using configuration parameters 
specified in the gp_init_config file of System Initialization Parameters.


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

gpinitsystem -c <gp_init_config_file> [-r | -B <parallel_processes>] 
[-h <host_file>] [-p <postgresql_conf_param_file>] 
{[-s <standby_master_host>] [-i]} [-o] [-m <max_connections>] 
[-b <shared_buffer_size>] [-e <superuser_password>] [-n <locale>] 
[-a] [-q] [-l <logfile_directory>] [-D] [-? | -v]


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The gpinitsystem script will create a Greenplum Database 
instance using the values defined in a configuration file. 
(See 'gp_init_config_example') Before running this script, make sure 
that you have installed the Greenplum Database software on all the 
hosts in the array.

The functionality of gpinitsystem is analogous to PostgreSQLs 
'initdb' utility, which creates the data storage directories, generates 
the shared catalog tables and configuration files, and creates a 
template database, which is a template used to create other databases. 

In a Greenplum Database DBMS, each database instance (the master and all 
segments) must be initialized across all of the hosts in the system in 
such a way that they can all work together as a unified DBMS. The 
gpinitsystem script takes care of initializing the database on 
the master and on each segment instance, and configuring the system 
as a whole.

Before running gpinitsystem, you must set the $GPHOME environment 
variable to point to the location of your Greenplum Database server 
installation on the master host.

This script performs the following tasks:

* Verifies that the parameters in the configuration file are correct.

* Ensures that a connection can be established to each host. If a host 
  cannot be reached, the script will exit.

* Displays the configuration that will be used and prompts for confirmation.

* Initializes the master instance.

* Initializes the standby master instance (if specified).

* Initializes the primary segment instances.

* Initializes the mirror segment instances (if configured).

* Configures the Greenplum Database system and checks for errors.

* Starts the Greenplum Database system.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-c <gp_init_config_file>

Required. The full path and filename of the array configuration file. 
See 'gp_init_config_example'.


-r 

Runs in serial mode. The default is to build segments in parallel.


-B <parallel_processes>

The number of segments to create in parallel. If not specified, the 
script will start up to 60 parallel processes depending on how 
many segment instances it needs to create.


-h <host_file>

Optional. The full path and filename of a file that contains the host 
names of all the segment hosts in the array. If not specified on the 
command line, you can specify the host file using the MACHINE_LIST_FILE 
parameter in the gp_init_config file.


-p <postgresql_conf_param_file>

Optional. The name of a file that contains global postgresql.conf parameter 
settings that you want to set for Greenplum Database. These settings will 
be used when the individual master and segment instances are initialized. 
For more information about the default server configuration parameters see 
the Greenplum Database Administrator Guide.


-s <standby_master_host>

Optional. If you wish to configure a backup master host, specify the 
host name using this option. You must have the Greenplum Database 
software installed and configured on the backup master host.


-i (do not start synchronization)

If a standby master host is configured, this will not start the 
synchronization process between the primary and backup master hosts. 
The default is to start the synchronization process.


-o (create master only)

Creates a master instance only. Does not configure any segment hosts.


-m <max_connections>

Sets the maximum number of client connections allowed to the master. 
The default is 25.


-b <shared_buffer_size>

Sets the amount of memory a Greenplum server instance uses for shared 
memory buffers. You can specify sizing in kilobytes (kB), megabytes 
(MB) or gigabytes (GB). The default is 125MB.


-e <superuser_password>

The password to set for the Greenplum Database super user. Defaults to 
'gparray'. You can always change the super user password at a later 
time using the ALTER ROLE command. Client connections over the network 
require a password login for the database super user account (gpadmin).


-n <locale>

Sets the default locale (character set encoding) for the Greenplum 
Database system. Defaults to en_US.utf8 (UNICODE). You must make sure 
that the locale you specify exists on your operating system 
(locale -a will show which locales are available). See 
'Character Set Support' in the PostgreSQL documentation for more 
information on the supported character set encodings.


-a (do not prompt)

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.


-q (no screen output)

Run in quiet mode. Command output is not displayed on the screen, 
but is still written to the log file.


-l <logfile_directory>

The directory to write the log file. Defaults to ~/gpAdminLogs.


-D (debug)

Sets log output level to debug.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.


-v (show script version)

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum of this script.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Initialize a Greenplum Database array:

gpinitsystem -c gp_init_config -e mypassword


Initialize a Greenplum Database array with an optional standby master host:

gpinitsystem -c gp_init_config -s host09 -e mypassword


Display the online help for the gpinitsystem script:

gpinitsystem -?


